ay that not all the  people in the south regard black people  as lower beings  but to our regret these are the majority  true to our rule to take into the  automobile people waiting for the  occasion on the road  at an isolated petrol station in north  carolina we picked up an 18 year old boy  from a ccc camp near washington  these camps had been built by president  roosevelt for unemployed young men  originally for six months  roosevelt hoped to end unemployment in  six months but  later when it became clear that it was  not so easy to end unemployment the  camps were left for an  indefinite period quite a cold rain  was pouring his story was ordinary  his father was a farmer the old man's  affairs were not so good the  boy graduated from a high school he  didn't have enough money to go to  college  he set out to look for a job he did not  find it  we were passing through a black village  you can tell a black man's house right  away from a white man's house  our fellow traveler said with a smile we  replied  how does it happen then that all the  black people are poor  i don't know about that is your father  acquainted with any black people  we know many and you treat them well of  course  would you ask such a black person to sit  down at the table with your family  the youth laughed no that's impossible  but why well because because black and  white people could not sit together at  the same table  but why not you must be from new york  said the young man to the southern mind  new york is the limit of free thinking  and radicalism  not only here but even in new york  itself about which the young boy from  the south talked with such horror  it is almost impossible to see a black  person in a restaurant  a motion picture theater or a church  unless of course he is there as a  servant a reporter  by law black people are free citizens of  the united states  yet in the south under various pretexts  they are deprived of the right to vote  and in washington itself and not alone  washington but in the very building  where the laws were written  the following occurred a black man by  the name of de priest was elected to  congress from the city of chicago  to the disaffection of the white  congressman he sat beside them  at the sessions of the house of  representatives  but that wasn't all this black man with  his black secretary  went to eat in the congressional dining  room he could not be turned out  and he paid no attention whatever to the  quiet demonstrations against him  finally they thought of an excellent way  out of the situation  they closed the dining room they closed  down  entirely the congressional dining room  just so that the black man would not be  able to eat  with the white people  and so after making ten thousand miles  we found ourselves  in the capital of the united states  washington with its comparatively low  government buildings its gardens its  monuments its broad streets  looks somewhat like vienna somewhat like  berlin somewhat like warsaw somewhat  like  all the capitals and only automobiles  remind one that this city is located in  america  here there is an automobile for every  two persons but there is not a single  permanent theatre  for the entire population of five  hundred thousand having  looked over george washington's house in  mount vernon having visited sessions of  congress and having been at the grave of  the unknown soldier  we discovered that there was nothing  else really to see  only the president remained to be seen  and in america  that is not so difficult  twice a week at 10 o'clock in the  morning the president of the united  states receives  journalists we managed to come to such a  reception  it took place in the white house  gradually about a hundred people  gathered  they smoked talked aloud and looked  impatiently at the small white door  behind which  apparently the president of the united  states was sequestered finally the door  opened and the journalists crowding each  other 